from review_schema import A

A('8:1.1','When Indian Flowers Bloomed in Distant Lands — Ahmedabad poster','R',{'Process':'Exhibition publicity graphic reproduced in the slide; the original printing or digital production process is not identified.','Composition & Motifs':'A pale central text panel sits between close floral textile details. Red title and date lettering pick up the textile’s red blossoms; museum and TAPI logos sit above.','Edition & Circulation':'The poster advertises the Kasturbhai Lalbhai Museum showing, 8 December 2024–13 April 2025, and gives a collection range of 1250–1950.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A contemporary promotional design uses historic trade-textile imagery as its frame.'},'museum-display maritime-networks',sig='economic/trade=The exhibition title makes textile circulation across distant markets its organizing subject.|visual/aesthetic=Textile detail becomes the graphic identity of the exhibition.',dist='The poster’s exhibition dates are not the dates of every pictured textile.|The Mumbai graphic on slide 6 gives a different venue and 1250–1850 range; keep the two records distinct.')
A('8:1.2','Floral textile detail — TAPI poster frame','T',{'Field & Border Organization':'Large red flowers, blue-green leaves and thin dark stems fill the cropped side strips. The central publicity panel interrupts the textile; no complete original field or border is visible.','Technique':'A trade textile is indicated by the poster context, but the cropped reproduction does not establish hand-painting versus block printing.','Dyes & Materials':'Red, blue-green and pale ground are visible; fibre and dye chemistry are not labelled.','Function & Circulation':'The image is used to promote a TAPI trade-textile exhibition; original use, maker and destination of this particular cloth are unknown.'},'floral-trade',sig='visual/aesthetic=Enlarged flowers provide continuity between historical textile and contemporary graphic design.',dist='The range 1250–1950 describes the exhibition, not a date securely assignable to this detail.',uncertain=['Specific textile identity, date and technique uncaptioned.'])
A('8:3.1','Interwoven Globe — entrance banner arrangement','E',{'Physical Arrangement':'A wide title strip crosses a grid of different textile patterns. Below it, garment mannequins and a small information panel sit against deep red sections; the display is recessed within an architectural frame.','What the Arrangement Communicates':'The juxtaposed patterns and garments introduce variety within a shared worldwide textile-trade story. This is a reading of the visible grouping.','Objects & Origins':'The banner names the worldwide textile trade, 1500–1800. Individual cloth and garment labels are too small to identify.'},'museum-display maritime-networks',sig='economic/trade=The entrance announces trade as the connection between varied textiles.|visual/aesthetic=Patchwork-like graphic organization presents diversity before the visitor enters.',dist='The decorative grid is an exhibition design; it is not one historical patchwork textile.',uncertain=['Individual entrance-display objects are not labelled legibly.'])
A('8:3.2,3.3,3.4','Interwoven Globe — gallery sightline','E',{'Physical Arrangement':'A large coat-of-arms hanging occupies the left wall beside a doorway. A framed crowned mother-and-child image hangs to the right. Through the central opening, a gold-coloured flowering-vase textile stands nearly on axis in the next room, with a small red garment further right. Low rails hold visitors back from the side works.','What the Arrangement Communicates':'The doorway lets viewers compare textile ornament with heraldic and devotional imagery across rooms. The juxtaposition suggests that cloth participates in several systems of identity; it does not itself identify a trade route for each object.','Objects & Origins':'The slide names the Met exhibition but does not provide readable labels for the three principal works. Every component’s indexed location is retained as evidence of the display.'},'museum-display floral-trade heraldry',sig='economic/trade=The exhibition context places these works within worldwide textile circulation.|religious/cosmological=The crowned mother-and-child image introduces devotional use into the comparison.|visual/aesthetic=An axial sightline connects differently framed objects.',dist='The three indexed components are different objects in one display arrangement, not duplicates of one textile.|Do not infer exact origins or maker names from the illegible labels.',uncertain=['Individual exhibited-object identities and origins remain unverified.'])
A('8:4.*;8:5.*','Asia in Amsterdam — entrance and gallery arrangements','E',{'Physical Arrangement':'At the entrance, large exhibition lettering fills a deep blue wall at right. Two decorated cylindrical objects occupy stepped blue niches along the route; an enlarged harbour scene covers the rear-left wall, with a floral hanging visible beyond it. In the smaller gallery view, a narrative tapestry spans the left wall, two tall glass cases stand on the floor, and framed pictures run along the right wall toward a doorway. In the long gallery, cabinets and textiles line the left, still-life paintings line the right, and central cases, paired tall vessels and a rear screen establish an axis under the skylight. The nearest right-hand still life visibly includes a blue-and-white ewer.','What the Arrangement Communicates':'Placing functional luxury objects beside pictures of luxury goods lets the viewer compare circulating material things with their painted representations. Sightlines between rooms extend that comparison beyond isolated cases. The quoted review asks how Asian imports shaped Dutch identity; that argument is distinct from the physical description.','Objects & Origins':'Asia in Amsterdam, Rijksmuseum, 2015–16, is the captioned exhibition. Individual titles and provenances are unreadable. The review mentions Bouke de Vries’s concluding works, but does not identify every photographed object as his.'},'museum-display trade-objects voc porcelain',sig='economic/trade=The exhibition connects Asian imports to the Dutch luxury economy.|regional-identity=The review challenges a self-contained national account of the Dutch Golden Age.|visual/aesthetic=Opposing walls and central cases compare representations with material objects.',dist='The display assembles multiple media and origins; do not call the entire gallery Dutch-made.|The nearby reference to Bouke de Vries is not an attribution of the visible historical vessels.|Twenty-five index rows are retained as components of the arrangement, not asserted to depict one repeated object.',uncertain=['Object-level labels and production origins in the gallery views are not legible.'])
A('8:6.1','When Indian Flowers Bloomed in Distant Lands — Mumbai graphic','R',{'Process':'Video title card/exhibition graphic, not a separately identified historic print. Original design-production technique is unknown.','Composition & Motifs':'Text fills the left and upper area; a large red flower with a curling dark stem occupies the right. TAPI and museum marks sit below and above.','Edition & Circulation':'The graphic names Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Vastu Sangrahalaya, Mumbai, and Masterworks of Trade Textiles 1250–1850. The slide supplies no exhibition date.'},'museum-display floral-trade',sig='economic/trade=Trade-textile circulation is made the theme of a contemporary museum presentation.|visual/aesthetic=An isolated textile flower becomes a recognisable exhibition emblem.',dist='This is a different venue-specific graphic from the Ahmedabad poster, whose range ends in 1950; do not silently merge the two events.')
A('8:7.*','The Cloth that Changed the World — ROM display','E',{'Physical Arrangement':'Two mannequins stand in a large foreground glass case: a dark patterned drape at left and a yellow garment behind it. A tall textile with dark rock-like forms rises centrally. Beyond, pale and red figured hangings occupy staggered panels; a large medallion textile hangs on a purple panel at right, beside a coat with sleeves extended. Angled labels lie low along the case front, which reads India Today / L’Inde moderne.','What the Arrangement Communicates':'Draped clothing, extended garments and flat hangings expose different textile functions. The modern-India label places living practice beside historical cloth within the photographed sightline.','Objects & Origins':'The caption names the Royal Ontario Museum’s Indian chintz exhibition, 2020–21. Individual objects’ dates and making locations are not readable.'},'museum-display chintz craft-agency',sig='social=Garment presentation and the India Today section connect cloth to bodies and living makers.|economic/trade=The exhibition treats Indian chintz as a globally circulating material.|visual/aesthetic=Flat and draped presentation reveal different aspects of pattern and scale.',dist='The photograph’s historical exhibition theme does not mean every garment in the India Today case is an early-modern original.',uncertain=['Individual object metadata unresolved.'])
A('8:8.1','Ramayana battle ceremonial textile — Southeast Asian market','T',{'Technique':'The video label identifies a ceremonial textile from coastal southeast India; the still alone does not reveal the full sequence of painting, mordanting or printing.','Field & Border Organization':'The crop contains large crowned archers facing right, surrounded by smaller energetic monkey-like fighters. Long bows and arrow-filled quivers create repeated diagonals across a pale ground. The complete borders are not shown.','Dyes & Materials':'Red-brown, dark outlines and pale ground are visible; fibre and specific dyes are not captioned in this still.','Function & Circulation':'The embedded label says 1700s, coastal southeast India, made for Balinese or Sulawesi markets. It explicitly separates production region and destination.'},'ramayana textile-circulation',sig='economic/trade=Indian production serves Southeast Asian demand.|religious/cosmological=The Ramayana narrative is given a ceremonial textile form.|visual/aesthetic=Scale separates principal archers from smaller surrounding fighters.',dist='Made in India does not mean made only for Indian use.|The label gives alternative destination markets; it does not establish one exact surviving ritual use.')
A('8:9.1,10.1,10.2,12.1','Belitung cargo — bowls arranged as waves around a dhow','E',{'Physical Arrangement':'Dozens of bowls tilt upward on slender transparent rods at graduated heights. The rods build a wave-like crest at right and lower troughs in front. A model sailing dhow sits centrally behind the foreground bowls, with visitors visible beyond the enclosure. The close view shows repeated but varied brown painted bowl designs.','What the Arrangement Communicates':'Repetition makes cargo quantity and serial production visible; the undulating heights and model ship restore an image of maritime transport. This is a curatorial reconstruction, not the recovered archaeological arrangement.','Objects & Origins':'The lecture describes Chinese Tang-period cargo from a merchant dhow wrecked near Belitung around 830, exhibited in Singapore. The model is interpretive display furniture; it is not the excavated hull.'},'museum-display belitung maritime-networks mass-production',sig='economic/trade=Many similar bowls make commercial scale visible.|social=Ordinary serial wares receive attention alongside exceptional treasures.|visual/aesthetic=Wave-shaped mounting connects ceramic cargo with its maritime journey.',dist='The modern rods are museum mounts, not part of Tang ceramic production.|Do not mistake the model dhow for the original wreck or transfer the ship’s origin to the ceramics.',compare=[{'target':'The Cross — Subodh Kerkar','relationship':'complementary','reason':'Slide 9 pairs recovered maritime cargo with an artwork involving oceanic material transformation.'}])
A('8:11.1','Underwater ceramics — shipwreck video still','F',{'Subject & Evidence':'Several large round and oval ceramic vessels lie tipped or partly buried on the seabed. Encrusted surfaces and green-blue water reduce detail.','Studio vs Documentary Context':'An underwater video still following the Belitung slide; the PDF gives only a video link and does not independently identify this exact dive site or every vessel.'},'belitung photographic-mediation',sig='visual/aesthetic=Underwater condition contrasts with cleaned and separately mounted museum objects.|economic/trade=The lecture sequence connects submerged cargo with maritime exchange.',dist='Adjacency to the Belitung discussion is contextual evidence; it does not independently prove that every photographed vessel belongs to that wreck.',uncertain=['Exact underwater site and objects not captioned.'])
A('8:13.1','Unidentified reclining figure on a beach','S',{'Subject & Narrative':'An oversized painted human-like figure lies on sand beside the sea, with two children standing to its left. The blank Left caption leaves the artwork unnamed.','Pose & Gesture':'The body is horizontal, with red torso, blue lower clothing and yellow-brown shoes. The head appears tilted upward; the index’s face-down description is not secure from this view.','Material & Technique':'A large constructed or modelled figure is visible; underlying material cannot be determined.','Placement':'The beach and children establish site and scale, unlike the indoor museum display paired at right.'},'ocean-contemporary',sig='visual/aesthetic=The large inert figure and nearby children create an unsettling contrast of scale and posture.',dist='Do not identify artist, title, refugee symbolism or an exact event from this uncaptioned image alone.',uncertain=['Artwork, maker, date, location and intended meaning unidentified.'])
A('8:13.2','Eros — Sleeping Cupid, Caravaggio','P',{'Composition':'A pale sleeping child lies diagonally across a dark field, with bent limbs and a dark wing rising behind the torso. Strong illumination isolates the body.','Palette & Pigment':'Warm flesh highlights emerge from brown-black shadow; the small installation photograph limits pigment and brushwork analysis.','Motifs & Hidden Details':'A curved bow-like object lies beside the reclining figure; fine attributes are partly obscured by distance and frame.','Perspective Convention':'Foreshortening gives the sleeping body volume within a compressed dark space.'},'chiaroscuro ocean-contemporary',sig='visual/aesthetic=Concentrated light gives the sleeping body a strong physical presence.|social=Its Lampedusa exhibition setting places the historical image into a contemporary dialogue, without changing its original subject.',dist='The paper boats on the floor are not depicted inside Caravaggio’s 1608 painting.',compare=[{'target':'Sleeping Cupid above paper boats — Lampedusa display','relationship':'complementary','reason':'Painting and modern floor arrangement participate in one exhibition encounter.'}])
A('8:13.3','Sleeping Cupid above paper boats — Lampedusa display','E',{'Physical Arrangement':'The framed Caravaggio hangs high in a dark blue wall recess under focused light. Numerous small folded paper boats spread irregularly across the floor beneath, separated from visitors by a low rope. A large vertical interval divides painting and boats.','What the Arrangement Communicates':'The elevated canonical painting and vulnerable-looking boats invite the visitor to connect historical art with a Mediterranean setting. The museum title supplies a framework of trust and dialogue; exact maker and intended meaning of the boats are not given.','Objects & Origins':'Caravaggio’s Eros of 1608 is captioned within the Lampedusa 2016 exhibition. The floor objects belong to the installation context.'},'museum-display ocean-contemporary',sig='social=The exhibition’s trust-and-dialogue framing connects museum viewing to present relationships across the Mediterranean.|visual/aesthetic=Vertical separation and focused illumination create a deliberate contrast between framed painting and dispersed floor objects.',dist='Paper boats are an installation element, not artefacts from the same date or maker as the Caravaggio.',compare=[{'target':'Eros — Sleeping Cupid, Caravaggio','relationship':'complementary','reason':'The display gives the painting a new spatial and thematic context.'}],uncertain=['Paper-boat creator and full curatorial statement not supplied.'])
A('8:9.2,14.1,15.1','The Cross — Subodh Kerkar','S',{'Subject & Narrative':'A cross with widening arm ends is densely encrusted with shells. Its recognizable outline survives while the surface becomes irregular and organic.','Material & Technique':'Pale cupped shells accumulate over a dark substrate, with several larger white shells below the crossing. The slide asks about collaboration with the ocean; it does not give immersion duration or a full construction recipe.','Placement':'Photographed on a plain pale wall, then repeated as The Oceanic Cross in a Museum of Goa video card.','Iconographic Attributes':'The cross is a Christian form, but no corpus of Christ is attached in this view.'},'ocean-contemporary conceptual-authorship christian-adaptation',sig='religious/cosmological=The cross supplies a recognizable religious form.|social=The lecture’s collaboration question broadens authorship beyond the artist’s hand.|visual/aesthetic=Marine accretion transforms a geometric symbol into a textured object.',dist='The Cross is an artwork dated 2019 in the lecture, not a securely identified ancient shipwreck relic.|The title does not establish a particular denomination or immersion procedure.',compare=[{'target':'Belitung cargo — bowls arranged as waves around a dhow','relationship':'complementary','reason':'The slide connects historical maritime objects and contemporary ocean-mediated making.'}])
for refs,title,desc in [('8:16.1','India’s First Biennale — Fort Kochi poster','A small white typographic poster is pasted alone onto a weathered wall beneath corrugated roofing. The prominent date reads 12/12/12.'),('8:16.2','It’s my Biennale — repeated campaign design','The same person holding a biennale sign appears on the top and bottom posters of a vertical stack on a graffiti-marked wall.'),('8:16.3','It’s my Biennale — alternate campaign portrait','The middle poster in the same wall stack shows a different person holding the campaign sign. Its wording repeats the claim It’s my Biennale.')]:
 A(refs,title,'R',{'Process':'Printed publicity posters documented photographically. Printing machinery and ink process are not specified.','Composition & Motifs':desc,'Edition & Circulation':'The placement on ordinary walls in Fort Kochi makes public promotion part of the biennale’s urban presence. Photographer credits are preserved separately from poster authorship.'},'poster biennale',sig='regional-identity=The campaign connects an international exhibition format with local civic space.|social=The first-person slogan presents the biennale as something residents can claim.',dist='A photographer’s credit identifies the documentation, not necessarily the poster designer.|The repeated top and bottom image is one design; the middle portrait is a distinct variant.')
A('8:17.1','The Ship of Tarshish — Prasad Raghavan','S',{'Subject & Narrative':'A boat-shaped sculptural hull carries a rectangular stack of small container-like boxes. Its title connects a contemporary cargo form with the lecture’s Biblical trade discussion.','Material & Technique':'Constructed installation with a dark angular hull and repeated box modules. Materials are not specified by the slide.','Placement':'The work stands inside a long rough-walled hall with repeated windows and exposed roof structure. The hull’s pointed end projects toward the viewer.','Iconographic Attributes':'The title is written on the hull; container forms make modern logistics visible.'},'maritime-networks ocean-contemporary biennale',sig='economic/trade=Ancient trade references are juxtaposed with modern container shipping.|religious/cosmological=The title introduces a Biblical frame.|regional-identity=The paired Vallarpadam port photograph anchors the comparison near Kochi.',dist='The installation is not an archaeological reconstruction of an excavated Biblical ship.',compare=[{'target':'Vallarpadam container terminal — lecture photograph','relationship':'parallel','reason':'The slide explicitly pairs contemporary shipping infrastructure with the sculptural container ship.'}])
A('','Vallarpadam container terminal — lecture photograph','F',{'Subject & Evidence':'A large modern container ship faces the viewer in water, with stacked coloured containers, dockside cranes and port infrastructure behind.','Studio vs Documentary Context':'News photograph used as a comparison for The Ship of Tarshish. The slide supplies the terminal name but no photographic date or named photographer.'},'maritime-networks ocean-contemporary',sig='economic/trade=The photograph demonstrates contemporary logistical infrastructure alongside the artwork’s historical references.',dist='The actual container ship and Raghavan’s installation are separate objects, not two views of the same vessel.',compare=[{'target':'The Ship of Tarshish — Prasad Raghavan','relationship':'parallel','reason':'Explicit side-by-side comparison.'}],slides='8:17')
A('8:18.1','Seated figure with a log — L. N. Tallur','S',{'Subject & Narrative':'A large pale seated figure supports an enormous dark log obliquely across its upper body and head area. A visitor stands at left with a raised hammer-like tool.','Pose & Gesture':'The figure sits with one hand on a knee; the log conceals its face, displacing the usual focal point of a devotional statue.','Material & Technique':'A textured wood-like cylinder contrasts with a pale smooth figure bearing visible seams or cracks. Exact materials and intervention instructions are not readable.','Placement':'The work is shown in a white gallery with a small wall text and tool ledge to the right.'},'conceptual-authorship ocean-contemporary',sig='social=The pictured visitor’s action suggests participation in the work’s material state.|visual/aesthetic=The disproportionate log disrupts the seated figure’s expected silhouette.',dist='The slide does not title this work Veni, vidi, vici; that caption belongs to the tile installation at right.',uncertain=['Individual title, date, materials and participation instructions are uncaptioned.'])
A('8:18.2','Veni, vidi, vici — L. N. Tallur','E',{'Physical Arrangement':'Two long inward-facing sloped banks of terracotta roofing tiles rise on either side of a narrow central passage. Above them, the building’s exposed trusses converge toward the far wall. Repeated horizontal tile courses amplify the depth of the hall.','What the Arrangement Communicates':'Tiles become an environment encountered bodily through a corridor, while the roof above repeats their architectural association. The lecture links Mangalore tiles with Swiss-German Christian connections from 1865 onward.','Objects & Origins':'Tallur’s 2012 site-specific installation uses Mangalore terracotta tiles and iron at Aspinwall House, Kochi-Muziris Biennale.'},'museum-display biennale mangalore-tiles',sig='regional-identity=A regional building material carries histories extending beyond the region.|economic/trade=The lecture locates tiles within transferred production and circulation.|religious/cosmological=The heading explicitly invokes Christian institutional connections.|visual/aesthetic=Repetition and a compressed path turn a roof material into immersive sculpture.',dist='The installation’s 2012 date and the historical 1865 connection are different chronologies.|The tiles’ relation to Christianity does not make the gallery a functioning church.')
A('8:19.1','Elephant by the shore — framed historical image','R',{'Process':'A framed coloured pictorial reproduction; print process and maker are not individually captioned. Classification as print follows the reproduced historical-image format and remains provisional.','Composition & Motifs':'A large elephant stands near water, with palm trees and tiny people surrounding it; two figures sit in the foreground. A pale mount and frame separate it from the contemporary installation view.','Edition & Circulation':'The slide’s heading connects the image to Elephanta, excavation and colonial legacy, without specifying this image’s catalogue record.'},'colonial-documentation ocean-contemporary',sig='visual/aesthetic=The intact elephant image provides a comparator for the fragmented form in Tallur’s installation.',dist='Do not assign the historical-looking image to Tallur solely because his installation is captioned beside it.',uncertain=['Original work identity, medium, maker and date unresolved.'])
A('8:19.2','Quintessential — L. N. Tallur','E',{'Physical Arrangement':'A partly cut-away elephant-shaped wooden form occupies the dark room, surrounded by many detached rectangular and curved blocks on the floor. A large four-part video display hangs behind and above; two pale busts on plinths flank the rear. A small framed image on the left wall echoes the separately enlarged historical picture.','What the Arrangement Communicates':'The juxtaposition of intact image, dismantled body, museum busts and surveillance-like video stages a tension between preserving, observing and fragmenting an object. This is an inference from the arrangement, connected by the heading to Elephanta and colonial legacy.','Objects & Origins':'Tallur’s Quintessential, 2012, is the lecture identification. The exact materials of every component and provenance of the busts are not supplied.'},'museum-display colonial-documentation ocean-contemporary',sig='political=The lecture explicitly asks viewers to reconsider colonial excavation and legacy.|visual/aesthetic=Fragmentation and live-looking screens complicate the stable museum object.|social=The gallery’s looking and recording apparatus becomes part of the encounter.',dist='The floor blocks are elements of a contemporary installation; they are not automatically original excavated fragments from Elephanta.|The video imagery is visible, but the still cannot verify that the feed was live.')
A('','Oceanic history and connected art histories','D',{'Argument':'The lecture places art history alongside histories of the Mediterranean and Indian Ocean, naming Braudel, K. N. Chaudhuri and Ashin Das Gupta. It asks how political, social and cultural networks can organize the study of art.','Evidence':'Trade-textile exhibitions, shipwreck cargo and contemporary installations supply cases of movement, serial production and changing object lives.','Course Throughline':'The ocean connects production, exchange, display and interpretation; it is both a historical route and an active contemporary medium.'},'connected-histories maritime-networks museum-display',dist='Connected history does not mean every object has an identical route or that cultural contact was always harmonious.',slides='8:1,2,3,4,7,9,10,14,17,18,19,20')
A('','Conceptual authorship and collaboration with the ocean','C',{'Definition':'An analytical term for the lecture’s question about Kerkar: artistic authorship may involve setting conditions for environmental growth or transformation, rather than personally making every visible mark. The term itself is supplied by the corpus brief; the slide asks the question of collaboration.','Nearest Confusable Terms':'Conceptual authorship is not absence of material work. Natural transformation, workshop collaboration and viewer participation distribute agency differently and require object-specific evidence.','Applied To':'The shell-encrusted Cross is the main case; the photograph of a visitor acting on Tallur’s log provides a distinct participatory comparison.'},'conceptual-authorship ocean-contemporary',dist='Do not infer a precise immersion method or duration from the concept alone.',slides='8:14,15,18')
A('','Kochi-Muziris Biennale and reclaiming global ties','D',{'Argument':'The lecture connects a contemporary biennale and its local public campaign to Kochi/Muziris’s longer trade histories. Artworks bring Biblical, industrial and colonial connections into present exhibition space.','Evidence':'Street posters, Raghavan’s container ship, Tallur’s tile environment and Quintessential address different scales of connection.','Course Throughline':'Regional identity is represented through outward connections and contested histories, not through isolation.'},'biennale maritime-networks connected-histories',dist='Reclaiming global ties is a curatorial-historical argument, not proof that every ancient maritime connection remained uninterrupted.',slides='8:16,17,18,19')
A('','Why reclaim oceanic ties today?','D',{'Argument':'The concluding slide asks what connectedness teaches us and places three earlier examples side by side. The visual recap invites a historical answer built from specific forms of exchange rather than a claim of pure, isolated traditions.','Evidence':'The Vijaya Vitthala warrior column, Ascetic Riding a Nag and Portrait of Père Tanguy recur from Modules 1, 3 and 7. They involve different media, periods and ways of recognizing cultural contact.','Course Throughline':'Oceanic ties illuminate trade, craft agency, political identities and artistic recombination. These dimensions remain separately arguable and need not all apply equally to every object.'},'connected-histories maritime-networks',dist='The recap does not erase differences between an architectural sculpture, a marbled-paper painting and a European portrait.|Connectedness is not a synonym for identical origin, technique or religious meaning.',slides='8:20')
